# Template for supabase.secrets — copy, fill in, keep local.
#
#   cp supabase.secrets.example supabase.secrets
#   chmod 600 supabase.secrets
#   $EDITOR supabase.secrets
#
# supabase.secrets is gitignored (*.secrets). It is meant to be *sourced* by
# tooling, never read out and pasted anywhere:
#
#   bash -c 'set -a; . ./supabase.secrets; set +a; tools/apply_migrations.sh "$SUPABASE_OWNER_URL"'
#
# You only need to fill in the first three. The role passwords are generated
# for you in step 6 of docs/SUPABASE_SETUP.md.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From the Supabase dashboard (see docs/SUPABASE_SETUP.md steps 1-4)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The project ref: the alphanumeric id in your dashboard URL,
# https://supabase.com/dashboard/project/<THIS-PART>
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=

# The Session pooler host, e.g. aws-1-ap-northeast-1.pooler.supabase.com
# Connect -> Session pooler. Copy only the hostname, no port.
SUPABASE_POOLER_HOST=

# The database password you chose when creating the project. This is the
# password for the `postgres` owner role. Shown once at creation time.
PGPASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived — no need to edit
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Owner connection, used only for migrations, backfill and pg_dump. Note there
# is deliberately no port and no sslmode in the URL: libpq lets an explicit URL
# value override the environment, so hardcoding them here would make PGPORT and
# PGSSLMODE dead settings.
SUPABASE_OWNER_URL=postgresql://postgres.${SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF}@${SUPABASE_POOLER_HOST}/postgres

# Through Supavisor every role carries the project ref as a suffix, custom
# roles included — so gym_writer connects as gym_writer.<project-ref>.
SUPABASE_WRITER_URL=postgresql://gym_writer.${SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF}@${SUPABASE_POOLER_HOST}/postgres
SUPABASE_READER_URL=postgresql://gym_reader.${SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF}@${SUPABASE_POOLER_HOST}/postgres

PGPORT=5432
PGSSLMODE=require
PGCONNECT_TIMEOUT=15

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated in step 6 — leave blank for now
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GYM_WRITER_PASSWORD=
GYM_READER_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the public page needs. Neither of these is a secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The page calls public.gym_heatmap() and public.gym_live() straight from the
# browser, so both of these end up in its source. The publishable key is the
# credential every Supabase browser client ships; it grants EXECUTE on those two
# functions and nothing else (see sql/migrations/005_public_api.sql). They live
# here only so a local `render_public_html.py` picks them up from the
# environment; on GitHub they are repository *variables*, not secrets.
#
# Dashboard → Project Settings → API. Newer projects show sb_publishable_...
# rather than a legacy anon JWT; either works.
#
# It must be the PUBLISHABLE (or legacy anon) key, never sb_secret_... and
# never a service_role JWT: those bypass every row-level security policy, and
# this value is written into a page on the open web. render_public_html.py
# refuses to build with one, and publish.yml refuses to deploy a page
# containing one -- but a published key can only be rotated, never recalled.
#
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY is still read as a fallback for older checkouts.
SUPABASE_URL=https://${SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF}.supabase.co
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub PAT that lets Supabase wake the collector
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only sql/supabase/001_dispatch_cron.sql reads this, to copy it into Supabase
# Vault. Once that has run, the database holds its own encrypted copy and this
# line matters again only when rotating.
#
# Create at https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens as a
# fine-grained token:
#   Repository access     only NTU_Gym_Traffic
#   Repository permissions  Actions: Read and write   (nothing else)
#
# It expires. Check the deadline any time with:
#   curl -sI -H "Authorization: Bearer $GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN" \
#     https://api.github.com/repos/Uranustrong/NTU_Gym_Traffic \
#     | grep -i github-authentication-token-expiration
GITHUB_DISPATCH_TOKEN=
